cules and in the examples i'm  going to be showing you now we're going  to be looking at diclofenac a very  well-known drug  we're going to be using a three  compartment or three organ system and  this system is interesting it's just  dermal exposure to primary human  hepatocytes to a different type of  hepatocyte over here to look at effects  of metabolites from liver 1 on liver 2.  so we're going to define non-specific  binding in a null system or a system  with no cells or tissues we're going to  look at the  cytotoxicity range so what's the maximum  polar dose we can put on without killing  our tissues  we want to confirm that the organs that  we want to be producing metabolites can  actually produce those metabolites  we need to develop the analytical system  in this case lc msms  and then finally we dose the system the  full system all linked together  and start collecting the data  again this is a dermal exposure just  showing you again the insert and the  dose is applied to the apical side  of the skin  and this is the first step we we are  just looking at toxicity or maximum dose  and then those on the dermal side  we're using a mtt assay and this fist  percent viability you can see not much  change in viability over 48 hours  in the liver we're using the primary  hepatocytes and here we see pretty  significant toxicity as the doses go up  uh expressed both in micromolar uh and  in the mass applied  and then at the bottom this shows the  development of the analytical process  and a nice  peak for diclofenac  now diclofenac  has as you  probably well know has several  metabolites the parent compound is shown  at the top left  that is hydroxylated the 4-hydroxy so we  get a hydroxylated diclofenac and it can  also be glucuronidated  so we wanted to be able to know if our  system could pick up these metabolites  over time  so the red bars on the left are showing  you the formation of the 4-hydroxy  diclofenac and you can see at the three  doses we're getting a nice increase  over time 1 6 and 24 hours  of the  metabolite now it's going down at 300  because we saw remember for in the last  slide we saw toxicity  and also the same is true with the  glucuronide again we can see the  glucuronic going up on a time dependent  manner and as toxicity occurs at the  higher dose of course it goes down  we also wanted to confirm that the  material moved across the skin so we  wanted to look at dermal absorption  and so again we can see here this is  showing you just using the the dermal  cup  to look at  the movement you can see a nice increase  over time and that allows us to  calculate permeability coefficients  and this is the beginning of the  estimates of bioavailability  so now we've got most of the prista  pre-study work completed  we we understand non-specific binding we  understand what doses we could  administer to the tissues without  causing detrimental effects we  can we've confirmed the production of  the metabolites that we want to monitor  and we've developed our anal political  method  so now we want to take that three  compartment system that i showed you a  couple of slides back which is consists  of the skin and the liver one and liver  two and we want to look at the  developing the kinetic curves that we  need to look at any type of ademi data  so this is diaclofanac dosed at 15  micrograms of mass on the apical surface  of the skin  and this is time on the y on the x-axis  and the amount of diclofenac recovered  on the y-axis and you can see  uh this is a two-dose exposure so we  dose at time zero we collect samples and  you see it nicely plateau right around  24 hours and at 24 hours it receives a  second dose again we get a rise much  like you would in vivo just as you would  expect  if we look at the other compartments now  the light yellow line uh represents the  simulated blood and you can see the  material went up quite rapidly in the  blood as you would expect in vivo as  well  and there is a slight delay as the  material moves across and into the  the  first liver which is in black and that's  lower has a lower amount of recovered  material than the red because we're  looking at parent compound and in the  black or the first liver we have much  higher rates of metabolism remember  these are the human primary hepatocytes  and the other cells are a cell line  and you can see  that that is reflective  in the material being delivered  and here we have a different dose so the  last the first dose i showed you right  here was at 15 and you can see it  plateaus around eight we increase the  dose to 30 and you can see now we've  really gone up again we're about 16 so  we're getting a nice dose response here  uh that's proportional to the dose given  um the same is true in the different  organ compartments um and we're also  seeing a nice time dependent increase in  the in the first liver compartment of  the glucuronide metabolite  again these kinetic curves are all being  generated in this plate system and all  of these data can be modeled  we look at delivery of metabolites to  the second liver compartment you can see  that the metabolites are or diclofenac  itself are causing a reduction  in total glutathione so that tells us  it's getting there we've measured it  there but it's also having a biochemical  effect  so now let's look at some other  compounds we looked at diclofenac now  i'd like to show you um  how we're moving towards trying to  estimate some of these pharmacokinetic  or add me parameters  so we're going to look at acetaminophen  and cyclohexamine we don't have time to  look at [ __ ] here today  but let's start with those  so here we have a three organ system  this time we're looking at oral  absorption so we're using the 3d human  intestine model  human hepatocytes and human  kidney cells  and we're looking for ways to calculate  auc c max and time to max  again we're looking at acetaminophen  which has low toxicity relative to  cyclohexamide an antifu